Wire NPU core0's MMU to the mainline rockchip-iommu driver.

Bring-up scope: core0 only. Registers from the vendor tree
(core_base + 0x2000/0x2100), rk3568-iommu compatible. Core1's banks
live in PD_NPU1 and are not listed - rockchip-iommu holds one power
domain per instance, and touching core1's banks with NPU1 down stalls
the interconnect; a second per-core node does not work either because
the driver supports one IOMMU instance per master. Jobs are pinned to
core0 until the driver can attach both domains.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah Peng <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576-armsom-sige5.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576-armsom-sige5.dts
@@ -1073,8 +1073,31 @@
 		power-domains = <&power RK3576_PD_NPU0>,
 				<&power RK3576_PD_NPU1>;
 		power-domain-names = "npu0", "npu1";
+		iommus = <&rknpu_mmu>;
 		status = "okay";
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * NPU core0 MMU (banks at core_base + 0x2000/0x2100), driven by
+	 * the mainline rockchip-iommu driver. Core1's banks live in
+	 * PD_NPU1 and are deliberately not listed: rockchip-iommu can
+	 * hold one power domain per instance, and touching core1's banks
+	 * with NPU1 down stalls the interconnect. Until the driver can
+	 * attach both domains, jobs must be pinned to core0
+	 * (rknn_set_core_mask). One instance per master is a mainline
+	 * limitation too, so a second per-core node does not work either.
+	 */
+	rknpu_mmu: iommu@27702000 {
+		compatible = "rockchip,rk3576-iommu", "rockchip,rk3568-iommu";
+		reg = <0x0 0x27702000 0x0 0x100>,
+		      <0x0 0x27702100 0x0 0x100>;
+		interrupts = <GIC_SPI 247 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		clocks = <&cru ACLK_RKNN0>, <&cru HCLK_RKNN_ROOT>,
+			 <&cru ACLK_RKNN_CBUF>, <&cru HCLK_RKNN_CBUF>;
+		clock-names = "aclk", "iface", "aclk_cbuf", "hclk_cbuf";
+		#iommu-cells = <0>;
+		power-domains = <&power RK3576_PD_NPU0>;
+	};
 };
 
